<.shell title="Chart" current={:chart}>
  <h1>Chart</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Charts are drawn on the server in HTML and SVG, with no JavaScript. They take their colours
    from the tokens, so they follow light and dark mode.
  </p>

  <h2>Columns</h2>
  <p>
    Counts over time, stacked by kind from the baseline. Use for activities by month. A
    column with one series needs no legend.
  </p>
  <div class="demo">
    <.column_chart
      id="sample-columns"
      columns={chart_columns()}
      series={[incident: "Incidents", exercise: "Exercises", event: "Events"]}
      caption="Activities by month"
    />
  </div>

  <h2>Lines</h2>
  <p>
    A running total against last year's. This year is primary with a wash under it and its
    value at the end; last year is grey. The line stops at today.
  </p>
  <div class="demo">
    <.line_chart
      id="sample-lines"
      labels={~w(Jan Feb Mar Apr May Jun Jul Aug Sep Oct Nov Dec)}
      series={chart_hours()}
      format={&(Service.Format.number(&1) <> "h")}
      caption="Member hours by month"
    />
  </div>

  <h2>Calendar</h2>
  <p>
    A year of days, a column per week, Monday at the top. 5 shades of one blue, from none to
    the busiest day. Hover a day for its date and count. It needs about 720px, so on a
    phone it scrolls sideways. The component does this itself; don't wrap it.
  </p>
  <div class="demo">
    <.calendar id="sample-calendar" days={chart_days()} />
  </div>

  <h2>Week grid</h2>
  <p>When things happen: a row per day, a column per hour. Use for when incidents start.</p>
  <div class="demo">
    <.week_grid id="sample-week" rows={chart_week()} />
  </div>

  <h2>Bar list</h2>
  <p>
    A ranked list, longest first, with the value at the bar's tip. Names link to the
    record. Use for most hours, or qualifications expiring.
  </p>
  <div class="demo" style="max-width: 560px">
    <.bar_list id="sample-bars" rows={chart_members()} />
  </div>

  <h2>Heat shades</h2>
  <div class="chart-scale" style="font-size: var(--fs-sm)">
    <span>Fewer</span>
    <i :for={level <- 0..4} class={"shade-#{level}"} style="width: 32px; height: 32px"></i>
    <span>More</span>
  </div>
  <p>
    <code>--shade-1</code> to <code>--shade-4</code>, over <code>--surface-alt</code> for none.
    Lightness changes in one direction in both modes.
  </p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Pick the form from the job. One number is a <a href={page_path(:stat)}>stat</a>, not a chart.
    </li>
    <li>
      Colour follows the thing, never its rank. Incidents, exercises, and events always wear
      their D4H colours. A single series is primary.
    </li>
    <li>
      Thin marks: columns at most 24px wide with a 4px rounded top, 2px lines, a 2px gap
      between stacked parts. Gridlines are 1px and quiet.
    </li>
    <li>
      A legend for 2 or more series. Labels and values stay in text colours, never a series colour.
    </li>
    <li>
      Hover shows the numbers, and every chart carries a hidden table of them, so nothing is
      only in a tooltip or only in colour.
    </li>
    <li>One y-axis. Two measures on different scales are two charts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>import Web.Components.Chart</code>. Styles are in <code>assets/css/components/chart.css</code>.
  </p>
</.shell>
